<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Thembu Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Thembu paramountcy of the eastern Cape, a senior Nguni royal house whose descendants include Nelson Mandela.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thembu">Thembu</a> were among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni-speaking</a> peoples settled between the Kei and Mthatha rivers, with royal genealogies reaching back many generations before written records. By the 18th century the Thembu paramount was recognised as holding senior rank among the southern Nguni, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondo">Mpondo</a> royal houses.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king ruled through councils of chiefs and counsellors, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> as the measure of wealth and the basis of marriage alliances. Royal houses were divided into the Great House and the Right-Hand House, a pattern shared with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/xhosa-kingdom">Xhosa Kingdom</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngubengcuka">Ngubengcuka</a> in the early 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thembu">Thembu</a> sought alliances with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> against raiders displaced by the wars of that era.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Missionary influence and Cape magistrates spread through Thembuland from the mid-19th century, and after conflicts under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngangelizwe">Ngangelizwe</a> the territory was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> by 1885. The royal house continues, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> was a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngubengcuka">Ngubengcuka</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/thembu-kingdom.jpg" alt="Thembu Kingdom" width="279" height="400"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Martin Duggan-Cronin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AbaThembu_Chief_of_the_Thembu_1900%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ngubengcuka, Mtirara, Ngangelizwe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Xhosa religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>isiXhosa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>abaThembu, Thembuland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=thembu-kingdom" aria-label="Thembu Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5279 3253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Thembu Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5470 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5599 3509h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5599 3509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Africa. <a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=thembu-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
